8 Nanorc Files
**************
Nanorc files can be used to configure ‘nano’ to your liking without
using command-line options.  

[Configuration File Location]
During startup ‘nano’ normally reads two
files: 
	first the system-wide file
		/etc/nanorc
	and then the user-specific file, either
		~/.nanorc
		$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nano/nanorc
		.config/nano/nanorc8
whichever exists first.  

[Custom Configuration]
However,
	if --rcfile is given, 
	‘nano’ skips the above files 
and reads just the specified settings file.

[Configuration File Contents]
A nanorc file can contain
	‘set’ and ‘unset’ commands for various options 
(*note Settings::)
It can also contain 
	commands that define syntax highlighting and 
	commands that rebind keys 
(*note Syntax Highlighting::)
(*note Rebinding Keys::)

[Configuration File Syntax]
Each command should be on a separate line,
and all commands should be written in lowercase.
Quotes inside the CHARACTERS parameters below should not be escaped.
The last double quote on the line will be seen as the closing quote.

[System vs User Configuration File]
Options that do not take an argument are unset by default.
So using the 
	‘unset’ command 
	is only needed when wanting to override a setting
	from the system’s nanorc file in your own nanorc.  
Options that take an argument cannot be unset,
but can be assigned the empty string.

Any command-line option overrides its nanorc setting, of course.
